FRM.registerQuestions(49, [{"id":"538","page":65,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"σt2 = 0.05 + 0.03μ 2 + 0.91σ(2t−1) (t−1)"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"σt2 = 0.03 + 0.03μ 2 + 0.92σ(2t−1) (t−1 )"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"σt2 = 0.07 + 0.02μ 2 + 0.94σ(2t−1) (t−1)"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"σt2 = 0.03 + 0.04μ(2t−1) + 0.91σ(2t−1)"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Given that σt2 is the estimated variance at time t and μt is the realized return at time t, select the GARCH(1,1) model that will take the longest time to revert to its mean."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To determine which GARCH(1,1) model will take the longest time to revert to its mean (or unconditional variance), we need to look at the persistence of the model, which is given by the sum of the coefficients on the lagged squared return (α1) and the lagged variance (β1). The closer this sum is to 1, the slower the model reverts to its mean."},{"type":"p","text":"Here are the sums for each choice:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Choice A: α1 + β1 = 0.03 + 0.91 = 0.94\nChoice B: α1 + β1 = 0.03 + 0.92 = 0.95\nChoice C: α1 + β1 = 0.02 + 0.94 = 0.96\nChoice D: α1 + β1 = 0.04 + 0.91 = 0.95"},{"type":"p","text":"Among these, the sum closest to 1, indicating the longest time to revert to the mean, is:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Choice C with a sum of 0.96."},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the GARCH(1,1) model from Choice C will take the longest time to revert to its mean."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. The sum of the ARCH term (0.03) and the GARCH term (0.91) in this model is 0.94, which is less than that in option C (0.96). This means that shocks to the variance will decay more quickly, and hence, the model will revert to its mean faster."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. Similar to choice A, the sum of ARCH and GARCH terms here is 0.95 which again indicates a quicker reversion to mean compared to option C."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. Despite having a higher ARCH term (0.04), this model's total persistence (sum of ARCH and GARCH terms = 0.95) remains lower than that in option C, implying a faster mean reversion time."},{"type":"pre","text":"Note.  In  a  GARCH(1,1)  model,  higher  values  of  μ  2       or  σ(2t−1 )  coefficients  indicate  slower  decay\n                                                         (t−1 )"},{"type":"p","text":"of shocks or longer persistence - thus taking longer for variance to revert back to its long-term"},{"type":"p","text":"average or 'mean'. Therefore, among all options provided here, choice C with highest combined"},{"type":"p","text":"value for these coefficients would take longest time for mean reversion."}],"remember":["GARCH(1,1) model stands for Generalized Autoregressive Conditional Heteroskedasticity model with 1 lag each for the autoregressive and moving average terms.","Persistence in a GARCH model is determined by the sum of the coefficients of the lagged squared return and the lagged variance.","A higher persistence value indicates a longer time for the model to revert to its mean.","The ARCH term in a GARCH model captures the impact of past squared returns on current volatility, while the GARCH term captures the impact of past volatility on current volatility.","Mean reversion refers to the tendency of a variable to return to its long-term average over time."]},{"id":"539","page":67,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.2"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"7.144"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.45"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.5"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Martin Scholes, FRM, estimates daily variance ht using the following GARCH model on daily returns rt:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Ht = α0 + α1r2(t−1) + βh(t−1)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where α0 = 0.004, α1 = 0.05, β = 0.93Approximate the long-run annualized volatility (Assume there are 252 trading days in a year)."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"In the GARCH(1,1) model, the long-run average variance rate is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"h=        α0  − β)"},{"type":"p","text":"(1 − α1"},{"type":"pre","text":"=   (1  −  0.004    0.93=)  0.20\n           0.05 −"},{"type":"p","text":"Taking the square root, this gives 0.45 for daily volatility."},{"type":"p","text":"To determine the annualized volatility, we have to multiply 0.45 by √252, where 252 is the average number of trading days in a year excluding weekends and holidays."},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, annualized volatility = 0.45 ∗ √252 = 7.144"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"540","page":68,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"If we assume that the long-run estimated variance remains unchanged as α and β increase, the volatility term structure predicted by the model reverts to the long-run estimated variance faster."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"If we assume that the long-run estimated variance remains unchanged as α and β increase, the volatility term structure predicted by the model reverts to the long-run estimated variance more slowly."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"If the current volatility estimate is above the long-run average volatility, we would expect the estimated volatility term structure to be downward-sloping."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"If the current volatility estimate is below the long-run average volatility, we would expect the estimated volatility term structure to be upward-sloping."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider the GARCH(1,1) model:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σt2 = ω + αμ2(t−1) + βσ(2t−1)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where α + β < 1Which of the following statements is INCORRECT regarding the volatility term structure predicted by the model above?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"In the GARCH(1,1) model, the sum of α and β represents the persistence of shocks to the variance. If this sum is close to 1, shocks to the variance will be highly persistent, meaning that the volatility term structure will revert to the long-run estimated variance more slowly, not faster. Therefore, increasing α and β would not result in a faster reversion to the long-run estimated variance, contrary to what the statement suggests."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. The statement contradicts the behavior of the GARCH(1,1) model. As α and β increase, assuming that the long-run estimated variance remains unchanged, the volatility term structure predicted by this model reverts to the long-run estimated variance faster, not slower. This is because higher values of α and β imply a greater weight being placed on recent volatility estimates in determining future volatility."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. While it's true that if current volatility estimate is above the long-run average volatility, we would expect a downward-sloping estimated volatility term structure; however this statement does not reflect any specific behavior related to changes in α and β parameters of GARCH(1,1) model which was asked in question."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. Similar to choice C, although it's correct that if current volatility estimate is below the long-run average we would expect an upward-sloping estimated volatility term structure; but again this statement does not reflect any specific behavior related to changes in α and β parameters of GARCH(1,1) model which was asked in question."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"541","page":69,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.04803"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0529"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1521"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1812"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager at Meridian Bank uses the exponentially weighted moving average technique to model the daily volatility of a security, with λ equal to 0.95. The current daily volatility estimate stands at 1.8%. On a certain day, the security registers a closing price of $10 and then $8 the following day. Determine the updated estimate of volatility:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The new variance forecast will be given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"λσ(2t−1)                              +  (1  −  λ)u  2     )\n                                                     (t−1"},{"type":"p","text":"where σn2 = variance estimate for day n σ(2t−1) = volatility estimate made at the end of day (t-2) for day (t-1) U(2t−1) = The most recent observation of the squared return (on day n — 1) = ( 81−010)2 = (−0.2)2 = 0.04 λ = exponential constant Thus, the new variance forecast is"},{"type":"pre","text":"0.95 ∗ 0.0182 + (1 − 0.95) ∗ 0.04 = 0.0023078"},{"type":"p","text":"Hence"},{"type":"pre","text":"Volatility = √0.0023078 = 0.04803"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"542","page":70,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"43.4"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"6.59"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"8.5"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"23.1"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a portfolio with 60% invested in asset Y and 40% invested in asset Z. The mean and variance of the return on Y are 0 and 49 respectively. The mean and variance of the return on Z are 1 and 84 respectively. Given that the correlation coefficient between Y and Z is 0.4, determine the portfolio volatility."}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"σp2  =  w  2  σy2  +  wz2σ  2  + 2wy  ⋅    wz  ⋅ Corr (y,    z)     ⋅  σy  ⋅ σz\n           y                z"},{"type":"p","text":"= 0.62 ∗ 49 + 0.42 ∗ 84 + 2 ∗ 0.6 ∗ 0.4 ∗ 0.4√(49 ∗ 84)"},{"type":"p","text":"= 17.64 + 13.44 + 12.32 = 43.40"},{"type":"pre","text":"Volatility = √43.40 = 6.59"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"544","page":70,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00027"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.000124"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.01114"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00567"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The decay factor used in an EWMA model is approximated to be 0.97. In addition, daily volatility is estimated to be 1%. Given that today’s stock market return is 3%, determine the new estimate of volatility using the EWMA model."}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"σn2   =  λ σ(2n −1)  +   (1  −  λ  )u  2\n                                       (n−1)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where λ = 0.97 and u(n−1) = 3% Thus, σn2 = 0.97 ∗ 0.012 + (1 − 0.97) ∗ 0.032 = 0.000097 + 0.000027 = 0.000124"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn = 0.01114 or 1.11%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"545","page":71,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0003"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.07132"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.02732"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.01732"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A generalized autoregressive conditional heteroskedastic (GARCH)(1,1) model has the following parameters: ω = 0.000003; α = 0.05; β = 0.94Determine the implied long-run volatility level."}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"The  long-run average  variance    =        ω          =  (1 −0.000.0050−030.94=)  0.0003\n                                      (1 − α − β)\n     The  long-run  volatility  =  ⎷( (1    ω  −  β))  =  √0.0003  =  0.01732\n                                          −α"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"546","page":72,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.007857"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"7.855e-05"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"6.4e-05"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"6.17e-05"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The dollar/sterling exchange rate at 5 P.M. yesterday was 0.78 and the most recent estimate of the daily volatility stands at 0.8%. The EWMA model used in the analysis has λ = 0.9. If the exchange rate at 5 P.M. today proves to be 0.775, find an estimate of the new daily volatility."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The proportional daily change is ln(0.775/0.78) = -0.00643. The current daily variance estimate = 0.0082 = 0.000064."},{"type":"pre","text":"New daily variance estimate = 0.9 ∗ 0.000064 + 0.1 ∗ 0.006432\n                                             = 0.0000576 + 0.000004109 = 0.0000617\n          New daily volatility = √0.0000617 = 0.007857 or 0.7857%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"547","page":73,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"6.5e-05"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0006"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"2.60005"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.05"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An analyst has interest in two assets, A and B. At the close of business yesterday, these assets had daily volatilities of 1.3% and 2.0% respectively. In addition, the assets were priced at $40 for A and $80 for B as at the close of business yesterday, and the estimated correlation coefficient between the two assets stood at 0.25. The EWMA model used by the analyst had λ = 0.95. Compute an estimate of the covariance between A and B."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Recall that:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Correlation  coefficient =             Cov(A, B)        B)\n                            (Volatility A ∗ Volatility"},{"type":"p","text":"This means that if we make the covariance the subject of the formula, we get:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Cov(A, B) = Correlation coefficient ∗ Volatility A ∗ Volatility B\n              = 0.25 ∗ 0.013 ∗ 0.02 = 0.000065"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"548","page":74,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00625"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.01954"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"6.56e-05"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.2589"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An analyst has interest in two assets, A and B. At the close of business yesterday, these assets had daily volatilities of 1.3% and 2.0% respectively. In addition, the assets were priced at $40 for A and $80 for B as at the close of business yesterday, and the estimated correlation coefficient between the two assets stood at 0.25. The EWMA model used by the analyst had λ = 0.95. Assuming that the prices of the assets today are $40.5 and $80.5, update the correlation coefficient."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Recall that:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Correlation  coefficient  =            C ov(A, B)       B\n                             Volatility A ∗ Volatility"},{"type":"p","text":"This means that if we make the covariance the subject of the formula, we get:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Cov(A, B) = Correlation coefficient ∗ Volatility A ∗ Volatility B\n               = 0.25 ∗ 0.013 ∗ 0.02 = 0.000065"},{"type":"p","text":"The return for A is 0.5/40 = 0.0125 Similarly, the return for B is 0.5/80 = 0.00625 The new covariance estimate 0.95 ∗ 0.000065 + 0.05 ∗ 0.0125 ∗ 0.00625 = 0.00006566 For asset A, the new variance estimate = 0.95 ∗ 0.0132 + 0.05 ∗ 0.01252 = 0.0001684 Therefore, new volatility = √0.0001684 = 0.01298 For asset B, the new variance estimate = 0.95 ∗ 0.022 + 0.05 ∗ 0.006252 = 0.000382 Therefore, new volatility = √0.000382 = 0.01954 The new correlation estimate = (00.0.010209086∗506.06195=4) 0.2589"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"556","page":75,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0125"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.41"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0001586"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0001482"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Robert Kelly, FRM, uses the EWMA model to carry out daily updates of correlation and covariance rates between two random variables X and Y . The weight for the most recent covariance on day n − 1 is 0.80. The correlation estimate between X and Y on day n − 1 is 0.6. In addition, on day n − 1, X and Y have estimated standard deviations of 0.013 and 0.019 respectively. Also, the percentage change on day n − 1 for variables X and Y are 2% and 1% respectively.Calculate the updated covariance between X and Y on day n."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) model uses the following formula to compute the updated covariance between 2 random variables X and Y at time n:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Covn = λ × covn−1 + (1 − λ)Xn−1 × Yn−1"},{"type":"p","text":"Where: λ = weight of the most recent covariance on day n − 1 Xn−1 = percentage change for variable X on day n − 1 Yn−1 = percentage change for variable Y on day n − 1 We know that Covxy = ρxy ∗ σxσy Covn−1 = 0.6 ∗ 0.013 ∗ 0.019 = 0.0001482 Which gives: Covn = 0.8 ∗ 0.0001482 + 0.2 ∗ 0.02 ∗ 0.01 = 0.0001586"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"557","page":75,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1586"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.2152"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.3088"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.6152"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Robert Kelly, FRM, uses the EWMA model to carry out daily updates of correlation and covariance rates between two random variables X and Y . The weight for the most recent covariance on day n − 1 is 0.80. The correlation estimate between X and Y on day n − 1 is 0.6. In addition, on day n − 1, X and Y have estimated standard deviations of 0.013 and 0.019 respectively. Also, the percentage change on day n − 1 for variables X and Y are 2% and 1% respectively.Compute the updated correlation between X and Y"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We know that ρxy = Co vxy"},{"type":"pre","text":"σx σy"},{"type":"p","text":"Updated variance of X = λσX2,n−1 + (1 − λ)Xn2−1 = 0.8 ∗ 0.0132 + 0.2 ∗ 0.022 = 0.0002152"},{"type":"p","text":"Updated variance of Y = λσY2,n−1 + (1 − λ)Yn2−1 = 0.8 ∗ 0.0192 + 0.2 ∗ 0.012 = 0.0003088"},{"type":"p","text":"The updated covariance between 2 random variables X and Y at time n:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Covn = λ × covn−1 + (1 − λ)Xn−1 × Yn−1"},{"type":"p","text":"Where: λ = weight of the most recent covariance on day n − 1 Xn−1 = percentage change for variable X on day n − 1 Yn−1 = percentage change for variable Y on day n − 1 We know that Covxy = ρxy ∗ σxσy"},{"type":"pre","text":"Covn−1 = 0.6 ∗ 0.013 ∗ 0.019 = 0.0001482"},{"type":"p","text":"Which gives:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Covn = 0.8 ∗ 0.0001482 + 0.2 ∗ 0.02 ∗ 0.01 = 0.0001586"},{"type":"p","text":"Hence the updated correlation coefficient is,"},{"type":"pre","text":"ρn =  0.0001586   = 0.615238\n      (√0.0002152 ∗ √0.0003088)"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"558","page":77,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1.51%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"11.11%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"3.33%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"12.27%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst uses daily data to estimate a GARCH (1,1) model as follows:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2 = 0.000002 + 0.16rn2−1 + 0.74σn2−1"},{"type":"p","text":"She also has established that the most recent return and variance are 0.04 and 0.02, respectively. Calculate the updated volatility."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Generalized Autoregressive Conditional Heteroskedasticity GARCH(1,1) model is defined as:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2 = ω + αr2n−1 + βσn2−1"},{"type":"p","text":"Where: σn2 = updated variance at time n ω = long-term average variance rate α = weight of the most recent squared return r2n−1= most recent squared return β= weight of the most recent variance rate estimate σn2−1 = most recent variance rate estimate From the information provided in the question:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2 = 0.000002 + 0.16 × 0.042 + 0.74 × 0.02 = 0.015058"},{"type":"p","text":"Volatility is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Volatility = √σn2 = √0.015058 = 0.12271 ≈ 12.27%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"565","page":78,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"9.07205"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1234"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.1416"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0001416"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A certain analyst uses the EWMA model with λ = 0.9 to carry out an update of correlation and covariance rates. On day n − 1, the observed percentage changes for variables X and Y are 3% and 2% respectively. Historical data puts the correlation estimate between X and Y at 0.54 on day n − 1. Furthermore, the estimated standard deviations on day n − 1 are 1.2% and 1.4% for X and Y respectively. Compute the new estimate of the covariance between X and Y on day n."}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"Covn = λ × covn−1 + (1 − λ)Xn−1 × Yn−1"},{"type":"p","text":"But first, we must determine Covn−1 We know that Covxy = ρxy ∗ σxσy"},{"type":"pre","text":"Covn−1 = 0.54 ∗ 0.012 ∗ 0.014 = 0.00009072"},{"type":"p","text":"And"},{"type":"pre","text":"C ovn = 0.9 ∗ 0.00009072 + 0.10 ∗ 0.03 ∗ 0.02 = 0.000141648"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"567","page":78,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.7"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"1"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.49"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The following is a variance-covariance matrix:"},{"type":"pre","text":"⎡ 1 0 0.7 ⎤\n⎢ 0 1 0.7 ⎥\n⎣ 0.7 0.7 1 ⎦"},{"type":"p","text":"Determine the correlation rate between variables 2 and 3."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Generally, a 3 × 3 covariance matrix is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"⎡ V ar(1)     C ov(1, 2)                     C ov(1, 3) ⎤\n⎢ C ov(1, 2)   V ar(2)                       C ov(2, 3) ⎥\n⎣ C ov(1, 3)\n              C ov(2, 3)                      V ar(3) ⎦"},{"type":"p","text":"Now,"},{"type":"pre","text":"Corr(2, 3) =    Cov(2                        ,S3d)(2)=   0.7  =  0.7\n              Sd(1) ×                                   1×1"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"940","page":79,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Asset returns have fat-tailed distributions which means assets have a higher probability weight in their tails relative to the normal distribution."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Asset returns have skewed distribution, which means that the declines in asset prices are more severe than increases in prices."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Asset returns have unstable parameter values due to varying market conditions."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Asset returns have symmetrical distributions which means they are evenly distributed around the mean returns."}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A number of risk measures are based on the parametric approach, which assumes that the asset returns are normally distributed. However, mathematicians and statisticians have discovered that in reality, the asset returns deviate from normality. Which of the following options is least likely consistent with the assumption that the asset returns deviate from normality?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The statement that 'Asset returns have symmetrical distributions which means they are evenly distributed around the mean returns' is least consistent with the assumption that asset returns deviate from a normal distribution. In a normal distribution, the data is symmetrically distributed with no skewness, meaning that the mean, median, and mode of the distribution are all the same. The data points are evenly distributed around the mean, creating a bell-shaped curve. However, in reality, asset returns often exhibit skewness and kurtosis, deviating from this symmetrical, bell-shaped curve of a normal distribution. Skewness refers to the asymmetry in the distribution, while kurtosis refers to the 'tailedness' of the distribution. Therefore, the assertion that asset returns have symmetrical distributions is least likely to be consistent with the real-world observations of asset return distributions."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. Empirical evidence and statistical analysis have shown that asset returns often exhibit fat-tailed distributions, which means they have a higher probability weight in their tails relative to the normal distribution. This is a characteristic of many financial market returns and aligns with the observed reality. Choice B is incorrect. Asset returns are often skewed, meaning that declines in asset prices can be more severe than increases in prices. This skewness is another common feature of financial market returns and aligns with the observed reality. Choice C is incorrect. It's true that asset return parameters can be unstable due to varying market conditions. This instability can lead to changes in volatility, skewness, kurtosis etc., which again aligns with the observed reality of asset return distributions."}],"remember":["Skewness in asset returns: Skewness refers to the asymmetry in the distribution of asset returns, where one tail is longer or fatter than the other. Positive skewness indicates a longer right tail, while negative skewness indicates a longer left tail.","Kurtosis in asset returns: Kurtosis measures the 'tailedness' of the distribution of asset returns. High kurtosis indicates heavy tails and a sharp peak, while low kurtosis indicates light tails and a flat peak.","Normal distribution assumptions: The normal distribution assumes that data is symmetrically distributed with no skewness or kurtosis. It is characterized by a bell-shaped curve where data points are evenly distributed around the mean.","Fat-tailed distributions: Fat-tailed distributions have a higher probability weight in the tails compared to a normal distribution. This implies that extreme events are more likely to occur than what would be expected under a normal distribution.","Market conditions impact on parameter values: Market conditions such as economic events, geopolitical factors, and investor sentiment can lead to unstable parameter values in asset returns, causing changes in volatility, skewness, and kurtosis."]},{"id":"941","page":80,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"York’s explanation regarding the unconditional distribution is incorrect."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"York’s explanation regarding the conditional distribution is incorrect."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"York’s explanation regarding the conditional and unconditional distribution is incorrect."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"York’s explanation regarding the conditional and unconditional distribution is correct."}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Donald York is a quantitative analyst at Brooklyn Investments Hub, a tech investment company based in New York. York brings 5 years of experience in quantitative and statistical analysis. In one of his explanatory articles, he mentioned that when the mean and standard deviation of asset returns are the same for any given time period, the distribution of returns is said to be an unconditional distribution. In contrast, if the mean is the same at any time while the standard deviation of the return change over time, the return distribution is referred to as a conditional distribution. Identify the correct option from the following."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Donald York's explanation regarding the conditional and unconditional distribution is correct. In the field of finance and investment, the distribution of asset returns plays a crucial role in risk management and investment decision-making. An unconditional distribution, as explained by York, is a type of probability distribution where the mean and standard deviation of asset returns remain constant for any given time period. This implies that the probability of achieving a certain level of return does not change over time. On the other hand, a conditional distribution is one where the mean remains constant, but the standard deviation of returns changes over time. This suggests that the volatility or risk associated with the asset returns can vary, depending on different conditions or factors. Both these concepts are fundamental to understanding the behavior of financial markets and making informed investment decisions. Therefore, York's explanation of both these concepts is accurate and aligns with the standard definitions used in finance and investment analysis."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. York's explanation of an unconditional distribution is correct. An unconditional distribution does indeed have a constant mean and standard deviation for any given time period, as it does not take into account any conditions or changes over time."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. York's explanation of a conditional distribution is also correct. In a conditional distribution, the mean remains constant while the standard deviation can vary over time, reflecting changes in volatility based on different conditions or events."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. As explained above, both explanations provided by York regarding unconditional and conditional distributions are accurate."}],"remember":["Unconditional distribution: Mean and standard deviation of asset returns remain constant for any given time period Conditional distribution: Mean remains constant, but the standard deviation of returns changes over time Understanding the distribution of asset returns is crucial for risk management and investment decision-making Volatility or risk associated with asset returns can vary in a conditional distribution Financial markets behavior and investment decisions are influenced by these distribution concepts"]},{"id":"942","page":82,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Unconditional distribution."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Regime-switching distribution."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Unconditionally lognormal distribution."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Conditional distribution."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An analyst is conducting fundamental and technical analysis on Pak-China Trading Co (PCTC) stocks. The analysis takes into account the stock’s daily returns based on the mean and volatility of returns. If PCTC returns have a mean of 9.56 bps/day, a high volatility of 14.25 bps/day and a low volatility of 6.18 bps/day, determine which of the following distributions best fits the characteristics of PCTC’s return distribution?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Regime-switching models allow for the possibility of more than one regime or state, with each regime having its own distinct statistical properties. In the context given, the two regimes are the high and low volatility states. As such, the best distribution that fits the characteristics of PCTC's return distribution is a regime-switching distribution. The high volatility of 14.25 bps/day suggests that the stock is risky in certain periods, while the low volatility of 6.18 bps/day suggests that it is relatively safe in other periods. A is incorrect. The unconditional distribution assumes that the distribution of stock returns remains constant over time. Given the stock's varying volatility (two distinct regimes), an unconditional distribution wouldn't capture this variability. C is incorrect. The unconditionally lognormal distribution assumes that stock returns are lognormally distributed at all times, without any variation in distribution parameters like volatility. Given the data, this doesn't fit since we have two distinct volatilities. D is incorrect. Conditional distributions relate to models where today's distribution is conditional on past information, such as in GARCH models. They focus on time-varying volatility, but not necessarily on distinct regimes like the scenario presented."}],"remember":["Regime-switching models allow for multiple regimes or states, each with its own statistical properties.","Unconditional distribution assumes a constant distribution of stock returns over time.","Unconditionally lognormal distribution assumes lognormal distribution of stock returns at all times.","Conditional distributions are based on past information and focus on time-varying volatility."]},{"id":"3301","page":83,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0028"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0194"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0262"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.053"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investment company uses RiskMetrics to calculate the volatility. The volatility for the previous day is 0.02, and yesterday's return is 10%. What is the updated volatility if λ = 0.97 is used?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Adaptive volatility using the RiskMetrics model can be found as:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σt2 = λσt2−1 + (1 − λ)r2t−1,t\n    = 0.97 ∗ 0.022 + 0.03 ∗ 0.12 = 0.000688"},{"type":"p","text":"However, remember that variance is volatility squared."},{"type":"pre","text":"σt = √0.000688 = 0.0262\n                                                                                                                                                  TM"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3302","page":84,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"2.86%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"3.49%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"2.50%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"3.21%"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A hedge fund manages risk by calculating future volatility using RiskMetricsTM to calculate the volatility. The volatility of the portfolio today is 3% per day and today's observed return is 1%. The conditional volatility estimate, assuming that λ = 0.9, is closest to:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The volatility according to RiskMetrics is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2 = (1 − λ) rn2−1 + λσn2−1"},{"type":"p","text":"Where λ = The weight given to the most recent variance estimate. rn2−1 = estimate of most recent squared return σn2−1 = Most recent estimate of variance From the information given,"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2 = (1 − 0.9) 0.012 + 0.9 × 0.032 = 0.00082"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus the volatility is"},{"type":"pre","text":"√0.00082 = 0.02863 ≈ 2.86%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3303","page":85,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"4.00%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"5.66%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"3.40%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"3.35%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A hedge fund manages risk by calculating future volatility using historical standard deviation. The portfolio performance in the past 5 days are 2% (n-1), 4% (n-2), 6% (n-3), 2% (n-4), and 10% (n-5), respectively. The hedge fund uses the historical standard deviation (moving average) method to calculate volatility. What is the volatility estimate?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"σn   =  ⎷  m  1  1   m   (rn−i  −  ¯r)2\n              −\n                    ∑\n                    i=1"},{"type":"p","text":"Where:"},{"type":"pre","text":"r¯ =  1    m\n      m\n          ∑ rn−i\n      1\n      5   i=1\n   =     (0.02  + 0.04   + 0.06  + 0.02      + 0.1)   =  0.048"},{"type":"p","text":"Now,"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn  =  √  1  [(0.02  −  0.048)2  +  (0.04  −  0.048)2  +  (0.06   −    0.048)2   +     (0.02  −  0.048)2  +  (0.1  −  0.048)2]  ≈  3.35"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3377","page":86,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.04%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2.50%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"1.50%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2.03%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The parameters of a generalized autoregressive conditional heteroskedastic (GARCH) (1,1) model are α = 0.05, β = 0.91. Long-run volatility is 0.80%. If estimated daily volatility is 2% and recent stock return is 3%, compute the new estimated volatility using the GARCH (1,1) model."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"γ = 1 − α − β = 1 − 0.05 − 0.91 = 1 − 0.96 = 0.04 Weighted long-run variance = ω = γ VL = 0.04 ∗ (0.008)2 = 0.00000256 New variance is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                  σn2 = ω + αun2−1 + βσn2−1\nV ariance = 0.00000256 + 0.05 ∗ 0.032 + 0.91 ∗ 0.022 = 0.00041156\nV olatility = √0.00041156 = 0.0203 = 2.03%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3378","page":87,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"2.06%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"2.03%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.04%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2.50%"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The decay factor of exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) model is estimated to be 0.95. If the estimated daily volatility is 2% and the recent stock return is 3%, compute the new estimated volatility using the EWMA model."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"New variance is given by"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2  =  (1  −  λ)u  2    +  λσn2−1\n                    n−1"},{"type":"p","text":"V ariance = 0.05 ∗ 0.032 + 0.95 ∗ 0.022 = 0.000425 V olatility = √0.000425 = 2.06%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3383","page":88,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"cannot be estimated"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00051"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00048"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00051555"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An analyst is trying to updates the estimated covariance by using the exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) model with λ = 0.91. The analyst has gathered the following relevant data."},{"type":"pre","text":"Estimated standard deviation on day n − 1 for variables X : 2%\nEstimated standard deviation on day n − 1 for variables Y : 3%\nThe correlation between them: 0.8\nThe percentage change on day n − 1 for variable X : 2.5%\nThe percentage change on day n − 1 for variable Y : 3.5%"},{"type":"p","text":"What is the updated estimated covariance between them?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Covariance between X and Y on day n– 1 :"},{"type":"pre","text":"Covn−1 = 0.8 ∗ 0.02 ∗ 0.03 = 0.00048"},{"type":"p","text":"Updated covariance using EWMA:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Covn = λCovn-1 + (1 − λ)X n−1Yn−1\n        = 0.91 ∗ 0.00048 + (1 − 0.91) ∗ 0.025 ∗ 0.035\n        = 0.00051555"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3384","page":89,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.825"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.8"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.91"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.2"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An analyst is trying to updates the estimated covariance by using the exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) model with λ = 0.91. The analyst has gathered the following relevant data."},{"type":"pre","text":"Estimated standard deviation on day n − 1 for variables X : 2%\nEstimated standard deviation on day n − 1 for variables Y : 3%\nThe correlation between them: 0.8\nThe observed return on day n − 1 for variable X : 2.5%\nThe observed return on day n − 1 for variable Y : 3.5%"},{"type":"p","text":"Given that the new Covariance between X and Y is 0.00051555, what is the updated estimated correlation between them?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Compute updated standard deviation using EWMA."},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2  =  λσn2−1  + (1  −  λ  )u  2\n                                n−1"},{"type":"p","text":"Updated variance for X = 0.91 ∗ 0.02 ∗ 0.02 + 0.09 ∗ 0.025 ∗ 0.025 = 0.00042025 Updated standard deviation for X = √0.00042025 = 0.0205 Updated variance for Y = 0.91 ∗ 0.03 ∗ 0.03 + 0.09 ∗ 0.035 ∗ 0.035 = 0.00092925 Updated standard deviation for Y = √0.00092925 = 0.030483602"},{"type":"pre","text":"Updated  correlation  =      Cov(X  ,Y )  )]  =  0.002.0050∗0501.0535054836=020.824993729  =  0.825\n                         [SD(X) ∗   SD(Y"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3385","page":90,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00041"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00048"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.08402"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.003961"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Suppose that an analyst has gathered the following relevant data for estimating updated covariance using the EWMA model:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Estimated standard deviation on day n − 1 for variables X : 2%\nEstimated standard deviation on day n − 1 for variables Y : 3%\nThe correlation between them: 0.8\nThe percentage change on day n − 1 for variable X : 25%\nThe percentage change on day n − 1 for variable Y : 35%"},{"type":"p","text":"Assuming that we choose λ = 0.96, what is the updated estimated covariance between variables X and Y using the EWMA model?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"Covn−1 = 0.8 × 0.02 × 0.03 = 0.00048"},{"type":"p","text":"Updated covariance using EWMA model is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Covn = (λ)Covn−1 + (1 − λ)Xn−1Yn−1\n        = 0.96 × 0.00048 + 0.04 × 0.25 × 0.35\n        = 0.0039608 ≈ 0.003961"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4661","page":90,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"10.25%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"5.6%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"11.45%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"12.56%"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A stock market investor records the stock price for five consecutive days as 20.20, 20.00, 21.20, 21.00, and 23.30. Estimate the daily volatility using the stock price returns."}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"The variance rate is given by:         σn2 =  1m      r2n\nRecall that return are calculated by:            ∑\n                                              m            −i\n                                                 i=1\n                                       ri  =  Si − Si−1\n                                                 Si−1"},{"type":"p","text":"The following table gives the consecutive returns:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Day Stock Price                   ri\n1         20.2                    −\n2         20.0           −0.0099\n3         21.2                    0.06\n4         21.0           −0.00943\n5         23.3           0.10952"},{"type":"p","text":"Now,"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                 σn2 =  1m      rn2\n                                           ∑\n                                        m            −i\n                                           i=1\n1 [(−0.0099)2       +  (0.06 )2  + (−0.00943)2       +   (0.10952 )2 ]  =  0.0031563\n5"},{"type":"p","text":"The volatility is the square root of the variance rate:"},{"type":"pre","text":"∴ σn = √0.0031563 = 0.056 = 5.6%"},{"type":"p","text":"Note: Your textbook in section 3.3 says the following: \"The usual formula for calculating standard deviations from sample data would give the volatility estimated for day n from the return on the m previous days [using m-1]. In risk management, we usually simplify this formula [by replacing] m - 1 by m.\""},{"type":"p","text":"Also, it gives an example on which you can see that we use 1/m as the actual number of days and not the number of returns."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4662","page":92,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0245"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0258"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0051"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00735"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a GARCH (1,1) model with ω=0.00005, α=0.025, and β=0.90, what is the value of long-run average volatility?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The formula for the updated variance rate under GARCH(1,1) model is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2 = αrn2−1 + βσ2n−1 + γVL"},{"type":"p","text":"Where VL: long-run average variance rate α: weight given to the most recent squared returns β: weight given to the previous variance rate estimate γ: weight given to long-run average variance rate VL can be defined as:"},{"type":"p","text":"ω ω 0.00005 VL = γ = 1 − α − β = 1 − 0.925= 0.0006667"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the long-run average volatility is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"√0.0006667 = 2.58%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4663","page":93,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0009"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0044"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0303"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0262"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The current volatility for stock prices is estimated to be 4% per day. Assuming the GARCH (1,1) model with ω=0.00005, α=0.025, and β=0.90, what is volatility estimate in 50 days?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"According to GARCH (1,1), the expected variance rate on day t is given by:"},{"type":"p","text":"σn2+t = VL + (α + β)t(σn2 − VL)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where VL: long-run average variance rate α: weight given to the most recent squared returns β: weight given to the previous variance rate estimate σn2: current volatility estimate Now,"},{"type":"pre","text":"VL  =       w\n       1−α−β\n            0.00005\n    =  1  − 0.025 −  0.90=                              0.00067\n                    T hus, σn2+50 = 0.00067 + (0.025 + 0.90)50(0.042 − 0.00067) = 0.0006889"},{"type":"p","text":"Expected volatility is the square root of the expected variance rate:"},{"type":"p","text":"σn+50 = √0.0006889 ≈ 0.00262"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4664","page":93,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.64"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"-0.64"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.78"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"-0.78"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investor notes that the closing stock price for asset A yesterday was USD 50, with a corresponding volatility if 2.5% per day. Similarly, the closing stock price for asset B was USD 30, with a corresponding volatility of 1.5% per day. Today, the stock price for asset A closed at USD 45, and that of B closed at USD 35. The correlation coefficient between the stocks A and B on close of trading yesterday was 0.55. Using the EWMA model with λ=0.85, what is the updated correlation coefficient between stocks A and B?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"According to the EWMA model,"},{"type":"pre","text":"s2n = (1−?)r2n−1+?sn2−1"},{"type":"p","text":"For stock A, a return is -0.10 (= 455−050) and that of B is 0.1667 (= 353−030). Therefore, updated volatility for stock A is:"},{"type":"pre","text":"sAn = √(1 − 0.85) × (−0.10)2 + 0.85 × 0.0252) = 0.04507"},{"type":"p","text":"And that of B is:"},{"type":"pre","text":"sBn = √(1 − 0.85) × (0.1667)2 + 0.85 × 0.0152) = 0.06603"},{"type":"p","text":"Now using the formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Corr(A,     B)  =  Cov(A,    B)\n                      sA sB\n?Cov(A,B) = Corr(A, B) × sA sB"},{"type":"p","text":"So the covariance yesterday was:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Cov(An−1,Bn−1) = 0.55 × 0.025 × 0.015 = 0.00020625"},{"type":"p","text":"The covariance is updated using the formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"                      covn =?covn−1 + (1−?)xn−1yn−1\n?Cov(An,Bn) = 0.85 × 0.00020625 + (1 − 0.85) × −0.10 × 0.1667\n                                   = −0.002325"},{"type":"p","text":"Using the formula Corr(A, B) = Cov(A, B) once again, the new correlation is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"                  sAsB\nCorr(An,Bn) = 0.0−405.00702×302.50660=3−0.7813"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"6248","page":96,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"They exhibit lighter tails and higher mean returns compared to the normal distribution."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"They demonstrate negative skewness and fat tails, indicating a higher probability of extreme negative events."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"They show perfectly symmetrical distribution with consistent variance over time."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"They are less volatile than predicted by a normal distribution, with stable mean and variance parameters."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst is reviewing the return distributions of various equity portfolios. It is commonly assumed that asset returns follow a normal distribution for simplicity and ease in modeling. However, empirical evidence often suggests otherwise. In what ways do actual asset return distributions typically deviate from the normal distribution?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Asset return distributions often deviate from the normal distribution by demonstrating negative skewness and fat tails. This means that there is a higher probability of extreme negative events than what is predicted by a normal distribution, making the returns more volatile and risky than a normal distribution would suggest. A is incorrect because actual asset return distributions generally exhibit fatter tails rather than lighter tails and do not necessarily have higher mean returns compared to a normal distribution. C is incorrect because asset return distributions are often not perfectly symmetrical and demonstrate volatility clustering, with periods of high and low volatility. D is incorrect because actual asset returns are typically more volatile than predicted by a normal distribution, with unstable mean and variance parameters over time."}],"remember":["Asset returns frequently show negative skewness, indicating that extreme negative returns are more likely than extreme positive returns.","Fat tails in the distribution imply a higher probability of extreme events compared to a normal distribution, impacting risk measure calculations like VaR and ES."]},{"id":"6249","page":97,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Stable market conditions leading to predictable returns."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"High-frequency trading causing frequent small fluctuations."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Instability in market parameters and increased likelihood of extreme losses."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Perfectly symmetrical distribution of asset returns."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"What is one of the primary reasons for negative skewness and fat tails in asset return distributions, and what implications does this have for risk management?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Negative skewness and fat tails in asset return distributions are often caused by the instability in market parameters, such as mean and standard deviation, resulting in a higher likelihood of extreme losses. This implies that traditional risk measures might underestimate the risk, making it critical for risk managers to account for these extreme events. A is incorrect because stable market conditions do not lead to negative skewness and fat tails. B is incorrect because high-frequency trading may lead to frequent small fluctuations but does not necessarily cause fat tails. D is incorrect because asset return distributions are not perfectly symmetrical, as indicated by negative skewness and fat tails."}],"remember":["Instability in market parameters, such as mean and standard deviation, contributes to negative skewness and fat tails in asset return distributions.","Risk managers must acknowledge and model these extreme events to avoid underestimating potential risks.","Fat tails imply higher probabilities of extreme losses, necessitating adjustments in traditional risk models."]},{"id":"6250","page":98,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Conditional distributions assume that asset returns are constant over time, whereas unconditional distributions assume varying asset returns."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Unconditional distributions assume constant mean and variance, while conditional distributions allow these to change with market conditions."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Conditional distributions are based on historical averages, while unconditional distributions use forward-looking estimates."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Conditional distributions use a static model, while unconditional distributions consider time-varying parameters."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst is analyzing a portfolio and needs to distinguish between conditional and unconditional distributions. The analyst knows that asset returns can follow these distributions under different market conditions. Which of the following best describes the difference between conditional and unconditional distributions?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Unconditional distributions assume that the mean and variance of asset returns are the same for any given day, regardless of market and economic conditions. In contrast, conditional distributions account for changes in mean and variance depending on varying market conditions, offering a more realistic reflection of the financial environment. A is incorrect because it reverses the assumption; conditional distributions account for varying returns while unconditional assume constancy. C is incorrect because both conditional and unconditional distributions can use historical data; the difference lies in how they adjust for market conditions. D is incorrect because it reverses the roles; conditional distributions consider time-varying parameters, whereas unconditional distributions use static parameters."}],"remember":["Unconditional distributions assume constant mean and variance for asset returns, ignoring market conditions.","Conditional distributions recognize changing mean and variance, reflecting different market conditions and giving a more accurate risk assessment."]},{"id":"6251","page":99,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It provides a constant mean for asset returns and ignores changes in volatility."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"It captures the occurrence of fat tails by considering both high and low volatility regimes with constant mean returns."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It averages out the extreme highs and lows in asset prices to provide stable return estimates."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It assumes that volatility is constantly high, thereby simplifying the analysis."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"How does regime switching enhance the understanding of asset return distributions, and why is it important in quantifying volatility?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Regime switching captures the occurrence of fat tails by considering multiple regimes that can have either high or low volatility while maintaining a constant mean for asset returns. This approach helps in resolving the fat tails problem and provides a more nuanced understanding of volatility in different market conditions. A is incorrect because regime switching considers changes in volatility, rather than ignoring them. C is incorrect because regime switching does not simply average out extremes but distinguishes between distinct regimes of volatility. D is incorrect because regime switching does not simplify analysis by assuming constant high volatility but instead accounts for varying volatility regimes."}],"remember":["Regime switching accounts for multiple market conditions, capturing periods of both high and low volatility, which gives a better picture of return distributions.","By considering different volatility regimes, regime switching addresses the fat tails problem more effectively than models assuming constant volatility."]},{"id":"6252","page":100,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"GARCH(1,1) assumes constant volatility over time, making calculations easier."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"GARCH(1,1) can capture the mean reversion property of volatility more effectively than EWMA."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA accounts for fat-tails in return distributions, making it superior to GARCH(1,1)."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA is less sensitive to new data and provides smoother volatility estimates."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A portfolio manager is considering two approaches to estimate conditional volatility for a portfolio: the Exponentially Weighted Moving Average (EWMA) and the GARCH(1,1) model. What is a key advantage of the GARCH(1,1) model over the EWMA model?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The GARCH(1,1) model captures the mean reversion property of volatility more effectively than the EWMA model. This means that GARCH(1,1) can model how volatility tends to revert to a long-run mean over time, making it useful for predicting future volatility under changing market conditions. A is incorrect because GARCH(1,1) does not assume constant volatility over time; it models changing volatility. C is incorrect because EWMA does not specifically account for fat-tails in return distributions; GARCH(1,1) provides a more robust framework. D is incorrect because EWMA is, in fact, more sensitive to new data with exponential weighting, and GARCH(1,1) provides better long-term volatility predictions."}],"remember":["The GARCH(1,1) model is effective in capturing mean reversion properties of volatility, useful for accurate long-term predictions.","While EWMA is simple and responsive, GARCH(1,1) offers a more comprehensive approach by incorporating historical variance and return data."]},{"id":"6253","page":101,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA uses historical price data, while GARCH(1,1) does not."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"GARCH(1,1) can model long-run variance levels, while EWMA cannot."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA assumes normally distributed returns, whereas GARCH(1,1) does not."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"GARCH(1,1) provides more accurate volatility estimates than EWMA."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst is assessing two different models for estimating conditional volatility: EWMA and GARCH(1,1). Which of the following best describes a key difference between these two approaches?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The GARCH(1,1) model captures the mean reversion property of volatility more effectively than the EWMA model. This means that GARCH(1,1) can model how volatility tends to revert to a long-run mean over time. As a result, it provides more accurate predictions of future volatility under changing market conditions. This makes GARCH(1,1) useful for financial analysts and traders who need reliable volatility forecasts to manage risk and make informed investment decisions. A is incorrect because both models primarily use historical price data. C is incorrect because both models can be implemented under various distributional assumptions. D is incorrect as the accuracy of each model can vary based on specific market conditions and the data used."}],"remember":["GARCH(1,1) (Generalized Autoregressive Conditional Heteroskedasticity) can model long-run variance levels, capturing the mean-reverting nature of volatility. It estimates current volatility based on past returns and past volatility, allowing for long-term volatility forecasting.","EWMA (Exponentially Weighted Moving Average) assigns exponentially decreasing weights to past observations, emphasizing recent data more than older data. While it reacts quickly to changes in market conditions, it does not account for long-term mean reversion."]},{"id":"6254","page":102,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA fails to incorporate recent volatility data and changes slowly over time."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA cannot adjust for new information quickly, providing outdated volatility estimates."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA does not account for mean reversion in volatility, reducing its accuracy during changing market conditions."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA incorporates too many external variables, making the model overly complex."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"In the field of financial econometrics, accurately modeling and predicting volatility is crucial for risk management and investment decisions. Among the various models available, the GARCH(1,1) model and the EWMA model are widely used. What is a critical limitation of EWMA in estimating conditional volatility compared to the GARCH(1,1) model?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"A critical limitation of the EWMA model is that it does not account for mean reversion in volatility. This oversight reduces its accuracy, particularly during periods of changing market conditions where volatility tends to revert to a long-run mean. The absence of this feature in the EWMA model means it cannot dynamically adjust to shifts in volatility levels, leading to less reliable forecasts. In contrast, the GARCH(1,1) model effectively captures mean reversion, allowing it to model how volatility returns to its long-run average over time. This capability makes GARCH(1,1) more adept at predicting future volatility under varying market conditions."},{"type":"p","text":"A is incorrect because EWMA does incorporate recent volatility data and responds quickly to new information."},{"type":"p","text":"B is incorrect because EWMA is generally sensitive to new information due to its exponential weighting mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"D is incorrect because EWMA is actually simpler and does not incorporate too many external variables."}],"remember":["EWMA (Exponentially Weighted Moving Average) assigns more weight to recent observations, allowing it to quickly reflect recent market changes. However, it does not account for mean reversion, a key behavior of financial volatility.","GARCH(1,1) (Generalized Autoregressive Conditional Heteroskedasticity) explicitly models mean reversion, providing a more accurate and reliable prediction of volatility over both short and long terms."]},{"id":"6255","page":103,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA assigns equal weights to all historical return data."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA assigns declining weights to older historical return data."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA weights historical return data equally."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"EWMA assumes that volatility is constant over time."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst wants to use the exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) approach to estimate the volatility of returns. What is a key characteristic of the EWMA model in estimating volatility?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The EWMA model assigns declining weights to historical return data, giving more importance to recent returns. This means that recent return data has a larger impact on the current volatility estimate, making the EWMA model sensitive to recent changes in market conditions. However, a critical limitation of the EWMA model is that it does not account for mean reversion in volatility. This reduces its accuracy, particularly during periods of changing market conditions where volatility tends to revert to a long-run mean. A is incorrect because EWMA does not assign equal weights; it assigns exponentially declining weights. C is incorrect because the weights in EWMA are not equal and are not solely based on the length of the observation period. D is incorrect because EWMA regularly updates volatility estimates with new data, reflecting changing market conditions."}],"remember":["The EWMA model gives greater weight to recent return data, making it more responsive to recent market changes.","The approach uses an exponential decay factor (λ) to determine the rate at which weights decline."]},{"id":"6256","page":104,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"2.5%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"1.95%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"2.25%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"2.07%"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager needs to determine the updated volatility for a stock using the EWMA approach. The estimate of daily volatility over the previous day is 2% and the return on the asset over the previous day is -3%. Given a smoothing parameter (λ) of 0.94, what is the updated volatility?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Using the EWMA formula for updating volatility:"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                        σn2 = (1 − λ)r2n−1 + λσn2−1\n                      New Volatility = √(1 − 0.94)(−0.03)2 + 0.94(0.02)2\n                                             = √(0.06)(0.0009) + (0.94)(0.0004)\n                                             ≈ 0.02073 or 2.07%.\nσn2: The updated variance for the next period (n).\nλ: The decay factor, which determines the weight given to past data.\nr2n−1: The squared return for the previous day, (n − 1).\nσn2−1: The variance for the previous day, (n − 1)."}],"remember":["The EWMA model updates volatility using the previous retuns and previous volatility estimate, weighted by the smoothing parameter.","The smoothing parameter (λ) determines the responsiveness of the volatility estimate to new data.","A higher λ value implies slower responsiveness, while a lower λ value means faster responsiveness to return changes."]},{"id":"6257","page":105,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It assigns equal importance to all historical data points."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"It captures the mean reversion property of volatility better than other models."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It provides a more responsive volatility estimate that quickly incorporates new information."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It assumes that volatility remains constant over long periods."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Despite having several disadvantages, the use of EWMA also comes with a number of positives. Which of the following is an advantage of using the EWMA model over simpler historical volatility methods?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The EWMA model provides a more responsive volatility estimate that quickly incorporates new information. This feature makes it suitable for dynamic financial markets where recent data may be more indicative of current conditions. By assigning declining weights to historical return data, the EWMA model ensures that recent returns have a larger impact on the current volatility estimate. This responsiveness allows the EWMA model to adapt swiftly to market changes, making it particularly useful in environments where volatility is rapidly evolving. A is incorrect because EWMA does not assign equal importance; it gives more weight to recent data. B is incorrect because mean reversion is better modeled by GARCH rather than EWMA. D is incorrect because EWMA does not assume constant volatility; it adapts to new data regularly."}],"remember":["The EWMA model adapts to new data quickly, making it preferable in volatile markets.","EWMA's weighting scheme ensures recent returns have a greater influence on volatility estimates."]},{"id":"6258","page":106,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"MDE assigns equal weights to all historical return data."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"MDE weights data based on the similarity between current and past conditions."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"MDE uses a linear weighting scheme where the weights decrease steadily over time."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"MDE assumes a constant volatility environment similar to EWMA."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Alternative approaches to weighting historical return data include methods like Historical Simulation and Multivariate Density Estimation (MDE). How does MDE differ from EWMA in weighting historical data?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Multivariate Density Estimation (MDE) weights historical return data based on the similarity between current and past conditions, rather than using a fixed exponential decay factor as in EWMA. This allows MDE to better capture patterns that are contextually similar to the current market environment. A is incorrect because MDE does not assign equal weights; it assigns weights based on similarity. C is incorrect because MDE does not use a linear weighting scheme. D is incorrect because MDE does not assume a constant volatility environment and differs fundamentally in its approach from EWMA."}],"remember":["MDE does not assume a constant volatility environment. Instead, it adapts to changing market conditions by adjusting weights based on the contextual similarity of past data.","In contrast, EWMA uses a predefined decay factor that may not accurately reflect the complexities and nuances of market behavior, potentially limiting its effectiveness in certain scenarios."]},{"id":"6259","page":107,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.2146"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0435"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0545"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.046"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst is using the GARCH (1,1) model to estimate the volatility of a financial asset. If the parameters of the model are: ω = 0.01, α = 0.05, and β = 0.90, and the last observed variance is 0.04, and the last observed return is 0.03 what is the estimated volatility for the next period?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The GARCH (1,1) model estimates the next period's volatility using the formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2 = ω + α × r(2n−1) + β × σ(2n−1)"},{"type":"p","text":"Where α and β are the weights given to the most recent squared return and the previous variance rate estimate, respectively. Substituting the given values, we get:"},{"type":"pre","text":"σn2 = 0.01 + 0.05 × (0.03)2 + 0.90 × (0.04) = 0.046045"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the estimated volatility (standard deviation) is"},{"type":"pre","text":"√0.046045 = 0.21458"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"6260","page":108,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The stock's volatility will revert quickly to its long-term average."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The stock's volatility will exhibit significant mean reversion and stabilize rapidly."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The shocks to the stock's volatility will be persistent and decay slowly over time."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"The stock's volatility will remain unaffected by past returns."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager estimates the volatility of a stock using the GARCH (1,1) model and discovers that the sum of the coefficients α and β (Where α and β are the weights given to the most recent squared return and the previous variance rate estimate, respectively) is very close to 1. What implication does this have for the stock's volatility behavior?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"If the sum of the coefficients α and β is very close to 1 in a GARCH (1,1) model, it means that shocks to the stock's volatility will be persistent and decay slowly over time. Such a scenario implies that volatility shocks have long-lasting effects on the stock's volatility. This means that any significant change in volatility will have a prolonged impact, causing the volatility to remain elevated or subdued for an extended period. In such a scenario, volatility shocks have long-lasting effects on the stock's volatility, as the model suggests that the current volatility is heavily influenced by past volatility and past shocks. Consequently, the volatility process becomes highly dependent on its history, making it more challenging to return to a long-run mean. This persistence can be particularly important for risk management and forecasting, as it implies that the effects of market events or economic changes can endure much longer than in models with less persistent volatility."},{"type":"p","text":"A is incorrect because a sum close to 1 indicates slow, not rapid, reversion to the mean."},{"type":"p","text":"B is incorrect because a higher sum suggests less mean reversion and slower stabilization."},{"type":"p","text":"D is incorrect as it misinterprets the GARCH model’s reflection of past return impacts on volatility."}],"remember":["In the GARCH(1,1) model, α represents the weight given to the most recent squared return (the immediate impact of new information or shocks on volatility).","β represents the weight given to the previous period's forecasted variance (how much past volatility influences current volatility)."]},{"id":"6261","page":109,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Multiply the 1-day variance rate by 30 and take the square root."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Multiply the 1-day volatility by 30 and take the square root."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Square the 1-day variance rate and multiply by 900."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Add up the daily volatility estimates for 30 days and average them."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst needs to estimate long-horizon volatility using the GARCH (1,1) model. How can the analyst extend the 1-day volatility estimate to a 30-day time horizon, given a daily variance rate of 0.000025?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To estimate long-horizon volatility, the variance over T days is calculated by multiplying the 1-day variance rate by T. Therefore, the 30-day variance rate is 0.000025 × 30. To obtain the 30-day volatility, take the square root of the variance: √(0.000025 × 30) ≈ 0.0274 or 2.74%. B is incorrect because the multiplication and square-root process must be applied to the variance rate, not directly to volatility. C is incorrect as this misinterprets the variance scaling law. D is incorrect because adding up volatility estimates is not the correct approach; using variance is necessary for accurate calculations."}],"remember":["GARCH(1,1) Model Components: α (Alpha): Measures the impact of recent returns on current volatility. A higher α indicates that recent price changes have a significant impact on volatility.","β (Beta): Represents the influence of past volatility on current volatility. A higher β suggests that past volatility has a strong influence on current volatility.","ω (Omega): A constant term that ensures the variance remains positive and defines the baseline level of volatility."]},{"id":"6262","page":110,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Volatility shocks have no lasting impact regardless of the shock size."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Volatility will revert to a long-term average, reducing dependence on short-term shocks over time."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Volatility will increase indefinitely with accumulating shocks."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Volatility is assumed to be constant, with no fluctuations over time."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"What is the implication of mean reversion in a GARCH (1,1) model for long-term volatility estimates?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"In a GARCH(1,1) model, mean reversion implies that although short-term shocks may significantly impact volatility, the volatility will eventually revert to a long-term average. This characteristic indicates that even after substantial market events that cause spikes or drops in volatility, the effect of these shocks will diminish over time. The mean reversion property ensures that volatility does not perpetually deviate from its historical average, reducing the lasting impact of these shocks. Instead, volatility exhibits a tendency to return to stable average levels, providing a more predictable and consistent measure of market risk over the long term. A is incorrect because short-term shocks do impact volatility temporarily. C is incorrect as accumulating shocks will not increase volatility indefinitely; they revert to the mean. D is incorrect because GARCH (1,1) explicitly models volatility fluctuation over time."}],"remember":["Mean reversion in a GARCH (1,1) model ensures that volatility will eventually return to a long-term average, regardless of short-term deviations.","This characteristic helps in maintaining stability in long-term volatility predictions."]},{"id":"6263","page":111,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.25"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0278"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.00025"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.001"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager is using the GARCH (1,1) model to project long-term VaR. If the current estimated daily volatility is 3% and the model parameters are ω = 0.00005, α = 0.15, and β = 0.80, calculate the long-term variance rate."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The long-term variance rate (VL) in the GARCH (1,1) model is calculated using the formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"VL  =  1    ω  −  β\n          −α"},{"type":"p","text":"Given ω = 0.00005, α = 0.15, and β = 0.80, we find:"},{"type":"pre","text":"V L = 1 −00.0.10500−50.80= 0.001"}],"remember":["The long-term variance rate in GARCH (1,1) is driven by the constants ω, α, and β and their sum’s relationship to 1.","Scaling daily GARCH estimates is crucial in projecting long-horizon VaR to maintain consistency with the desired time period."]},{"id":"6264","page":112,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Implied volatility reflects the market's expectation of future volatility, while historical volatility relies solely on past data."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Implied volatility assumes constant volatility over time, whereas historical volatility is more dynamic."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Implied volatility does not depend on option prices for its calculation."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Historical volatility is unaffected by market sentiments compared to implied volatility."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An analyst wants to evaluate the effectiveness of implied volatility as a predictor of future volatility compared to historical volatility estimates. What is one significant advantage that implied volatility has over historical volatility?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Implied volatility reflects the market's expectation of future volatility, as it is derived from the prices of current options in the market. This measure captures the collective outlook of market participants on how volatile they believe the underlying asset will be over the life of the option. Because it incorporates traders' forecasts and sentiments, implied volatility is inherently forward-looking. In contrast, historical volatility is based solely on past price data of the asset, providing a backward-looking measure of how volatile the asset has been over a specified period. This reliance on historical data means that historical volatility does not account for current market conditions or future uncertainties."},{"type":"p","text":"B is incorrect because implied volatility does not assume constant volatility; it changes based on market conditions."},{"type":"p","text":"C is incorrect as implied volatility is indeed derived from option prices."},{"type":"p","text":"D is incorrect because historical volatility is not influenced by market sentiments, while implied volatility is."}],"remember":["Implied volatility is forward-looking, incorporating market expectations and sentiments into future volatility predictions.","Historical volatility, rooted in past return data, lacks the predictive insight provided by implied volatility."]},{"id":"6265","page":113,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Implied volatility can differ significantly across different options for the same underlying asset."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Implied volatility is based on historical price data and may not reflect current market conditions."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Implied volatility assumes that future volatility will be lower than historical volatility."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Implied volatility is influenced by market supply and demand for options, which can distort the true expectation of future volatility."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Which of the following is a shortcoming of using implied volatility as a predictor of future volatility?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"One shortcoming of using implied volatility is that it can differ significantly across different options for the same underlying asset. This variation, known as the \"volatility smile\" or \"volatility skew,\" can lead to inconsistent volatility estimates depending on which option’s prices are used for calculation. Consequently, relying on implied volatility from a single option might not provide a fully accurate or consistent measure of expected future volatility, making it challenging to obtain a unified view of market sentiment."},{"type":"p","text":"B is incorrect. Implied volatility heavily incorporates market sentiment, affecting its accuracy during unstable periods."},{"type":"p","text":"C is incorrect. Implied volatility does not inherently assume that future volatility will be lower (or higher) than historical volatility. It simply reflects the market's expectations of future volatility, which can be higher, lower, or similar to historical levels."},{"type":"p","text":"D is incorrect. While it is true that implied volatility can be influenced by market supply and demand for options, this is not considered a shortcoming specific to implied volatility."}],"remember":["Implied volatility can vary significantly between different options on the same underlying asset, creating inconsistencies.","This variability introduces challenges in using implied volatility as a reliable predictor of future market movements."]},{"id":"6266","page":114,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It assumes that implied volatility is a better predictor than historical volatility."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"It fails to account for the dynamic nature of market conditions, leading to potential inaccuracies."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It leads to volatility estimates that are always lower than expected."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It negates the influence of underlying asset prices on volatility prediction."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A portfolio manager is considering using implied volatility from current options trading on a stock to predict future volatility. How does the assumption of constant volatility over time represent a limitation in this context?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Assuming constant volatility over time is a limitation because it fails to account for the dynamic nature of market conditions. Volatility often changes due to various economic, political, and market events, and the assumption of stability can lead to inaccurate predictions of future volatility. A is incorrect because the assumption of constant volatility over time does not directly relate to the comparison between historical and implied volatility. C is incorrect because the assumption results in volatility estimates that can be either higher or lower than expected, but not consistently lower. D is incorrect because implied volatility does consider the influence of underlying asset prices, derived from option market data."}],"remember":["The assumption of constant volatility overlooks the inherent variability in market conditions, leading to potential inaccuracies in predictions.","Using implied volatility for predictions requires acknowledging its inherent limitations due to market fluctuations and sentiment variations."]},{"id":"6267","page":115,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Use a fixed correlation value calculated years ago for consistency."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Apply a rolling window approach where the correlation is recalculated using the most recent data periodically."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Estimate the correlation once and assume it remains constant indefinitely."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Assume zero correlation when new data is inconsistent with past trends."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager uses historical data to estimate the correlation between two assets in a portfolio. Given new market data, what is one method the manager can employ to update the correlation estimate?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"A rolling window approach allows the risk manager to update correlation estimates periodically using the most recent data. This method reflects current market conditions better and quickly incorporates new trends or changes in relationships between assets. A is incorrect as using a fixed correlation value ignores recent market dynamics, leading to outdated estimates. C is incorrect because correlations should be updated to reflect changing market conditions, not assumed constant indefinitely. D is incorrect because assuming zero correlation disregards the actual relationship between assets, impacting risk assessment quality."}],"remember":["The rolling window approach recalculates correlation using the most recent data, updating estimates to reflect current market conditions.","This method helps in capturing dynamic changes in asset relationships, improving the accuracy of risk assessments."]}]);
